            If you shoot deep-sky astrophotography on a DSLR or mirrorless camera and don't want to pay $300+ for commercial licenses like PixInsight, two names dominate the community: <strong class="text-white">DeepSkyStacker (DSS)</strong> and <strong class="text-white">Siril</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            For over fifteen years, DeepSkyStacker was the uncontested rite of passage for every beginner. But over the last few years, Siril has seen rapid development, introducing native 32-bit floating point pipelines, photometric star-color calibration, and automated script execution that rival premium commercial suites.
          </p>
          <p>
            Which one should you use for your shoots? Let's break down the technical differences.
          </p>

