          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>1. Calibration Math &amp; Color Calibration</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most striking difference between the two tools is what happens <em class="text-neutral-200">after</em> alignment:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">DeepSkyStacker's Approach</h3>
          <p>
            DSS is purely a calibration and registration engine. Once it computes your master stack, it gives you a raw 16-bit or 32-bit TIFF file and a basic RGB histogram with three sliders. Most experienced imagers leave the sliders completely untouched in DSS and immediately export the raw result into Photoshop, GIMP, or Siril for actual stretching.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Siril's Approach</h3>
          <p>
            Siril is not just a stacker; it is a comprehensive astronomical image processing suite. Its flagship feature is <strong class="text-white">Photometric Color Calibration (PCC)</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Siril plate-solves your image using the coordinates of your target and your focal length.</li>
            <li>It queries the official Gaia or NOMAD star catalogs, analyzes the exact B-V color index of hundreds of cataloged stars in your framing, and calculates a mathematically true white balance.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            This eliminates the endless guesswork of trying to manually balance green and blue airglow against faint H-alpha nebulae.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>2. File Organization: Where the Workflows Diverge</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            How each program intakes your camera's SD card is completely different:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">DeepSkyStacker: The Multi-Group File List</h3>
          <p>
            In DSS, you click <em class="text-neutral-200">"Open picture files"</em>, browse to your directory, and select your lights. Then you click <em class="text-neutral-200">"Dark files"</em> and select your darks. If you shot across multiple nights, DSS offers "Main Group" and numbered group tabs at the bottom.
          </p>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] p-4 text-sm space-y-1.5 my-4">
            <div><strong class="text-emerald-400">Pros:</strong> You don't have to physically move files into specific folders on your hard drive.</div>
            <div><strong class="text-rose-400">Cons:</strong> If your SD card has 400 photos with lights, darks, flats, and biases dumped together in sequential order, you still have to manually pick out which file numbers belong to which group every single time you stack.</div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Siril: The Strict Subdirectory Script Pipeline</h3>
          <p>
            Siril achieves its speed and repeatability through automated scripts (like <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">OSC_Preprocessing</code>). Instead of asking you to click buttons in a UI, the script expects your working directory to contain four exact folders:
          </p>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#0b101b] p-5 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-neutral-200 overflow-x-auto shadow-inner my-4">
<pre class="leading-relaxed"><code>working_directory/
├── lights/
├── darks/
├── flats/
└── biases/</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] p-4 text-sm space-y-1.5 my-4">
            <div><strong class="text-emerald-400">Pros:</strong> Once your files are in those folders, you click <em class="text-neutral-200">"Scripts &rarr; OSC_Preprocessing"</em> and walk away. Siril automatically builds master darks, master flats, debayers, registers, normalizes, and stacks without a single extra click.</div>
            <div><strong class="text-rose-400">Cons:</strong> If a single dark frame is missing or accidentally left inside <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1 py-0.5 rounded">/lights</code>, the automated script aborts with an error.</div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>3. Which Tool Should You Choose?</span>
          </h2>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] p-5">
              <div class="font-bold text-white text-base mb-3 border-b border-white/10 pb-2">Choose DeepSkyStacker if:</div>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-neutral-300">
                <li>You are on Windows and only want a simple, no-frills tool to merge 20 light frames of a bright target like the Orion Nebula or Andromeda Galaxy.</li>
                <li>You do 100% of your post-processing, stretching, and color correction in Adobe Photoshop, PixInsight, or Affinity Photo.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#00FFFF]/30 bg-[#101726] p-5">
              <div class="font-bold text-[#00FFFF] text-base mb-3 border-b border-white/10 pb-2">Choose Siril if:</div>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-neutral-300">
                <li>You are on macOS or Linux (DSS has no native build for non-Windows operating systems).</li>
                <li>You want access to modern astronomical tools: Photometric Color Calibration, Generalized Hyperbolic Stretch (GHS), and seamless StarNet++ star removal.</li>
                <li>You shoot faint emission nebulae from light-polluted suburbs where background gradient extraction is mandatory.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>The Unified Workflow: Pre-Sort Once, Use Both</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            The good news is that you don't actually have to choose one or the other. If you organize your camera card into clean calibration subdirectories (<code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/lights</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/darks</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/flats</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/biases</code>):
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>You can run <strong class="text-white">Siril's 1-click automated scripts</strong> with zero risk of script abort errors.</li>
            <li>You can drag those same organized folders into <strong class="text-white">DeepSkyStacker</strong> in two seconds without clicking through individual file ranges.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            This is why we built <a href="/software/astroplot" class="text-[#00FFFF] underline hover:text-white transition-colors">AstroPlot RAW</a>. It reads your raw EXIF metadata in parallel across all CPU cores and plots your shoot on a 2D scatter plot. In under 60 seconds, you can marquee-select your groups, exclude cloudy frames, and export clean calibration subfolders with full 1-click undo safety.
          </p>
